// input() stays a call that blocks its worker (spec/05-stdlib.md, Blocking
// calls). Standard input holds a line when the program starts, so the call does
// not wait here, and the probe cannot show a wait. It checks that the call
// returns its line in a task that shares its worker with another task, and that
// the other task runs after it.
// This probe does not show that input() blocks the worker: it passes the same
// when input() suspends its task while standard input has no line. No test of
// any backend holds that property: native has no unit test for input() in a
// task, and the JVM, QBE and bootstrap backends have none.
// STATUS: ok
// TIMEOUT: 10
// COVERS: blocks-call input in reader
// CHECK: input: line one
// CHECK: ticker ran
var me = task.self();
fun reader() { print "input: " + input(); task.send(me, "done"); }
fun ticker() { print "ticker ran"; task.send(me, "done"); }
task.spawn(reader);
task.spawn(ticker);
task.receive();
task.receive();
